## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers when using `CancelAfter` |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` deep in the stack** | Only catch it at the top orchestration point |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | `Handle(item)` won't be interrupted mid-execution — pass `token` into it if it does long work |
| **Original race condition** | The `ManualResetEvent` + single `Dequeue` pattern drops items if multiple `Enqueue` calls fire before `Dequeue` runs. Both replacements above fix this. |
| **`Thread.Interrupt` only worked on `WaitSleepJoin` state** | If the thread was in `Handle()`, `Interrupt` did nothing until the *next* blocking call — the original `Stop()` was already subtly broken |

---

## Summary of replacements

| Original | Replacement |
|----------|-------------|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` / `BlockingCollection.CompleteAdding()` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `while (true)` + `WaitOne` | `await foreach` / `GetConsumingEnumerable(token)` |
| Manual `lock` around queue | Not needed — both replacements are thread-safe internally |
